---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/sequenzy-vs-privy';
const title = 'Sequenzy vs Privy for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Sequenzy and Privy for Shopify capture, email, SMS, campaign controls and total operating cost, with account availability checks.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can either product run Shopify campaigns?",
    "answer": "Sequenzy documents Shopify sync, campaigns and flows; Privy advertises capture, email and SMS. Confirm installation and enabled features in your account: the Privy Shopify listing currently reports that the app is unavailable."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Sequenzy limited to email?",
    "answer": "No. Its pricing lists email-volume billing with unlimited contacts, web and iOS/Android push, and in-app messages. SMS/MMS is a paid-plan US/Canada beta add-on; sender approval and account eligibility still need verification."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Privy only a popup tool?",
    "answer": "No. Privy advertises email and SMS campaigns and automations as well as targeted popups, forms and quizzes. Product advertising does not establish that a new Shopify installation or every account can use each feature."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which fits a drop calendar?",
    "answer": "Choose after testing inventory changes, purchase exits, queue cancellation and recipient eligibility. Either may fit if its available account features pass those tests; no third sender is required by this comparison."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can capture consent be reused across channels?",
    "answer": "Do not infer SMS or push permission from an email signup. Preserve channel-specific permission, disclosure version, source, timestamp and withdrawal history, and validate identity mapping before activation."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should purchase exits work?",
    "answer": "Use Shopify order IDs and customer identity to cancel pending prospect messages after purchase. Test delayed and duplicate events, guest checkout and refunds; do not assume a segment refresh retires an already queued send."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should a migration preserve?",
    "answer": "Export permission and suppression history, map identifiers and event ownership, pause old automations, retire old queues and deduplicate retries. Reconcile pending sends before enabling a replacement, with a tested rollback owner."
  },
  {
    "question": "How do costs compare?",
    "answer": "Match eligible audience, email volume, SMS segments and destination mix. Include subscription, credits, overages, carrier fees, integration, staffing and migration. Privy lists mailable-contact email billing and page-view capture pricing; Sequenzy bills by email volume."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which produces better margins?",
    "answer": "Public feature pages cannot establish incremental profit. Compare matched eligible-customer holdouts over the same period using net sales less merchandise, fulfillment, messaging, app and operating costs, counting discounts and returns once."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Sequenzy vs Privy</nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="badge">Campaign marketing comparison</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Sequenzy vs Privy for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Compare available account features against your capture and campaign jobs. Sequenzy combines email-volume billing with Shopify automation and additional channels. Privy advertises capture, email and SMS, but new-install availability must be checked first.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl"><TldrBox picks={[
      { label: 'Sequenzy', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Consider for email-volume economics and Shopify flows; validate paid US/Canada SMS/MMS beta eligibility.' },
      { label: 'Privy', tool: 'Privy', reason: 'Consider for capture plus email/SMS if installation and required account features are available.' },
      { label: 'Decision gate', tool: 'Account pilot', reason: 'Prove consent mapping, order exits, queue retirement and matched total cost before migration.' },
    ]} /></div>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Current product scope and availability</h2>
      <p>Reviewed 25 November 2026: <a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">Sequenzy pricing</a> lists billing by emails sent with unlimited contacts, web and iOS/Android push, and in-app messages. Its paid-plan SMS/MMS beta uses a US/Canada toll-free number. This beta qualification applies to SMS/MMS, not the entire product. The <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/sequenzy">Shopify listing</a> describes commerce sync and automated flows. Verify storefront events and mobile SDK or in-app implementation separately; a Shopify installation alone does not establish mobile delivery.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/pricing">Privy pricing</a> advertises email and SMS campaigns, automations, segmentation and targeted capture, including quizzes. Email pricing uses mailable contacts with unlimited email sends; capture-only pricing uses page views. Its <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/privy">Shopify listing</a> currently says the app is not available. Confirm the installation route and account access with Privy before planning a new Shopify deployment. This listing does not establish that existing accounts have stopped operating.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Sequenzy</th><th>Privy</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Audience economics</td><td>Model email volume and paid SMS credits.</td><td>Model mailable contacts, SMS credits and capture page views.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Capture and messaging</td><td>Forms, popups, email and flows; other channels need their own permissions and setup.</td><td>Targeted displays, email and SMS advertised; confirm enabled account scope.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Launch gate</td><td>Confirm Shopify event mapping and SMS sender approval.</td><td>Resolve installation availability before campaign design.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Operational fit</td><td colspan="2">Test purchase exits, cancellation, identity and retries with actual account capabilities. Public pages do not prove these controls.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <h2>Fourteen fictional proposed merchant fixtures</h2>
      <p>Every merchant below is a fictional proposed test fixture, not a customer, endorsement or reported result. The jobs preserve distinct campaign decisions without inventing revenue, conversion gains or rankings. Run only the fixtures relevant to your store in each eligible account and record failures as well as passes.</p>
      <h3>Ember Ceramics: Kiln waitlist priority</h3>
      <p>Propose preserving waitlist order for a small kiln release. Reserve only sellable stock, pause the queue on fulfillment exceptions and cancel a buyer’s remaining invitations by order ID.</p>
      <h3>Ridge Run Coffee: Subscriber collision prevention</h3>
      <p>Propose excluding active subscribers from acquisition flash offers. Reconcile subscription status and Shopify purchases before each dispatch; route uncertain status to service rather than sending a discount.</p>
      <h3>Cipher Audio: Education before a launch</h3>
      <p>Propose teaser email followed by an optional launch alert for separately permitted engaged browsers. Approve product claims and test a no-discount holdout; do not treat browsing as SMS consent.</p>
      <h3>Patchwork Denim: One-of-one style matching</h3>
      <p>Propose preference-based alerts for unique pieces. Lock each item against duplicate invitations, refresh availability before dispatch and retire messages when the piece sells.</p>
      <h3>Arena Energy: Athlete collaboration permissions</h3>
      <p>Propose flavor-drop timing for an opted-in interest segment, with an email-only gift offer. Obtain creative and athlete usage approval and prevent a reward from leaking into channels with different promises.</p>
      <h3>Loom & Frame: Edition scarcity evidence</h3>
      <p>Propose collector alerts only after checking current edition inventory. A named operator approves scarcity wording and stops queued messages if a stock correction invalidates the claim.</p>
      <h3>Driftwood Surf Co: Seasonal restock relevance</h3>
      <p>Propose matching boardshort restocks to size requests while keeping forecast education separate. Expire old requests and exit purchasers before later restock waves.</p>
      <h3>Noir Collective: VIP early-access calendar</h3>
      <p>Propose a scheduled VIP access window before a general release. Verify access rights server-side, assign one calendar owner and cancel general prospect messages for VIP buyers.</p>
      <h3>SoleDrop ATX: Size-run allocation</h3>
      <p>Propose a sneaker waitlist per size with reservation expiry. A retry must reuse its allocation and message key; expired reservations return to the queue only after reconciliation.</p>
      <h3>Festival Threads Co: City and venue timing</h3>
      <p>Propose tour-date messages based on volunteered city preference and recipient timezone. Set an approved frequency budget, retire cancelled venue dates and route timezone uncertainty to a safe sending window.</p>
      <h3>Vinyl Vault Records: Collector value and access</h3>
      <p>Propose combining pressing interest with reconciled order history for early access. Separate net customer value from gross order count and avoid disclosing private collector status in creative.</p>
      <h3>Bloom & Wick: Gift deadlines and education</h3>
      <p>Propose holiday gift segments with verified shipping cutoff dates, alongside scent education for self-purchasers. A fulfillment owner approves deadline changes and withdraws obsolete queued reminders.</p>
      <h3>Peak Trail Gear: Clearance offer restraint</h3>
      <p>Propose clearance offers to an eligible bargain-interest segment with a matched holdout. Check contribution after incentive cost before expanding to full-price loyalists; no uplift is assumed.</p>
      <h3>Sauce Lab: Batch inventory and restraint</h3>
      <p>Propose batch-specific launch and low-stock alerts only when inventory supports the message. Cap frequency across channels and stop retries after purchase, opt-out or batch withdrawal.</p>
      <h2>Permission, identity and Shopify order truth</h2>
      <p>Keep channel-specific consent source, timestamp, disclosure version, destination and withdrawal history. Email permission does not grant SMS or push permission. Join guest checkout and known profiles using verified identifiers; quarantine ambiguous merges. Preserve channel-specific opt-outs and global do-not-contact requests without inventing consent for another channel. Review applicable sending rules and recipient timezone restrictions with a qualified owner; neither platform label establishes legal compliance.</p>
      <p>Use Shopify order and line-item IDs as purchase truth, reconcile late events and test repeated events, cancellations and refunds. Define which purchases end which prospect campaigns. Immediately before dispatch, recheck eligibility, inventory and suppression; verify that purchase exits also cancel scheduled messages. Refunds must not automatically restart acquisition messaging.</p>
      <h2>Queues, approvals and service handoff</h2>
      <p>Assign one owner per campaign and a stable deduplication key per customer, campaign and message step. Test retry after timeout without duplicate sends. During migration pause old automations, export pending sends, retire the old queues and reconcile counts before activating the replacement. Suppression imports must finish before messages can leave either system.</p>
      <p>Require approval for audience, offer, inventory claims, sender identity, creative rights and schedule. Confirm role permissions and audit history in the actual plan. Route replies, HELP requests, delivery failures and order exceptions to a named service team with a response target. If the account cannot provide a required approval or handoff, document an operator control or reject that workflow.</p>
      <p>Keep a versioned flow export, reconciliation log and rollback owner. A rollback pauses the replacement, retires its pending queue, restores suppression changes and reconciles sent IDs before the old flow resumes. Rehearse this sequence with test profiles so restoration cannot replay completed steps.</p>
      <h2>Matched total economics</h2>
      <p>Quote both options for the same eligible audience, monthly and peak email volume, destination mix and billable SMS/MMS segments. Include credits, overages, carrier charges, sender setup, capture tier, integration work, staffing, support and amortized migration. Push and in-app implementation also consume operating resources. A second platform is justified only when a tested requirement needs it and its total cost is included.</p>
      <p>For matched holdout groups over the same attribution and return window, calculate contribution as net sales less merchandise, fulfillment, payment, messaging, app and operating costs. Net sales already deduct discounts and returns: do not subtract them again. Compare incremental contribution per eligible customer against the holdout, then compare platforms on that same denominator. Message-level attributed revenue alone does not demonstrate lift.</p>
      <p>Use current written quotes and account limits rather than a stale headline price. Repeat the model at peak traffic and projected audience growth. See <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent controls</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> for operating worksheets.</p>
      <h2>Conditional choice</h2>
      <p>Sequenzy may fit when email-volume economics and its available Shopify flows match the work, with eligible SMS/MMS beta access where needed. Privy may fit when its capture and available email/SMS tools meet the work and installation access is resolved. Keep a functioning existing account if migration costs and control gaps outweigh the tested benefit. Neither needs an automatic companion stack or earns a universal campaign ranking.</p>
      <p>These public sources establish advertised scope, not account permissions, deliverability, queue behavior or merchant outcomes. Validate those in a limited pilot with a holdout and a rollback plan before expanding.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Sequenzy vs Privy FAQ" />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
